      "note": "'such' carries both the comparison of כְּ ('like') and the demonstrative הַהוּא ('that'); one English word for both, fused under rule 30 across the intervening noun, headed on the governing preposition (rule 23). The written relative אֲשֶׁר is what the 'as' of 'such ... as' introduces the clause with, so it takes 'as' under rule 11 rather than כְּ. Competing reading (the earlier accepted one) gave 'as' to כְּ, 'such' to הוּא alone and left אֲשֶׁר unrepresented.",
